WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:11.500
Reconnect antiderivatives, the constant of integration, and the FTC.

00:11.500 --> 00:23.000
Keep one habit: differentiate every answer to verify it.

00:23.000 --> 00:32.000
Differentiation turns x cubed into three x squared.

00:32.000 --> 00:41.000
Integration asks which function produced that derivative.

00:41.000 --> 00:50.000
An antiderivative F satisfies F prime equals f.

00:50.000 --> 00:59.300
Vertical shifts have the same slope at every x value.

00:59.300 --> 01:08.600
Differentiation destroys the constant information.

01:08.600 --> 01:18.000
An indefinite integral restores it with one constant C.

01:18.000 --> 01:27.300
The integral sign gives the operation; f is the integrand.

01:27.300 --> 01:36.600
The differential d x names the variable of integration.

01:36.600 --> 01:46.000
No bounds means an indefinite integral and a plus C.

01:46.000 --> 01:56.600
Move the right edge: a thin strip of area appears.

01:56.600 --> 02:07.300
Area change divided by width approaches the curve height.

02:07.300 --> 02:18.000
Therefore A prime of x equals f of x.

02:18.000 --> 02:28.600
A definite integral equals F of b minus F of a.

02:28.600 --> 02:39.300
The source slide plus sign is a typo: use subtraction.

02:39.300 --> 02:50.000
The constants cancel, so the final answer needs no C.

02:50.000 --> 03:00.000
Raise the exponent, then divide by the new exponent.

03:00.000 --> 03:10.000
The rule excludes n equals negative one.

03:10.000 --> 03:20.000
One over x integrates to log absolute x plus C.

03:20.000 --> 03:31.300
Indefinite: integrate three x squared to x cubed plus C.

03:31.300 --> 03:42.600
Differentiate the result to recover three x squared.

03:42.600 --> 03:54.000
Definite: upper minus lower gives nine minus one, or eight.

03:54.000 --> 04:03.000
Why does an indefinite integral include C?

04:03.000 --> 04:12.000
What does Fundamental Theorem Part One say?

04:12.000 --> 04:21.000
How do you evaluate a definite integral?

04:21.000 --> 04:30.000
Recognize the pattern. Reverse the derivative. Verify the result.
